1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,720
The factory model of machine learning is

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:06,210
an allegory or a framework that I think describes

3
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:12,010
well how to bootstrap a system into high-performance with data.

4
00:00:12,110 --> 00:00:15,930
The factory really is a mapping between data

5
00:00:15,930 --> 00:00:20,360
set and outcome a model you want that does your job.

6
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:22,905
But it's usually not quite so simple.

7
00:00:22,905 --> 00:00:25,335
Typically, the process is cyclical.

8
00:00:25,335 --> 00:00:29,190
You start by collecting a data set on a task you want,

9
00:00:29,190 --> 00:00:31,995
send part of it for labeling.

10
00:00:31,995 --> 00:00:35,505
Take these labels and create a model.

11
00:00:35,505 --> 00:00:38,900
Evaluate this model, find shortcomings,

12
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:45,385
and decide potentially what other data needs to be collected or labeled.

13
00:00:45,385 --> 00:00:50,490
Perform that, collect this version 2 of the data set and repeat.

14
00:00:50,490 --> 00:00:55,820
This is a environment and infrastructure in a pipeline that once established,

15
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:59,575
can keep churning models for you and close this virtuous loop.

16
00:00:59,575 --> 00:01:03,710
In typical industry we see over many generations,

17
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:06,385
your models keep getting better and better and better.

18
00:01:06,385 --> 00:01:11,300
Even though it takes a non-trivial investment in setting up all this infrastructure,

19
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:13,800
that it does a lot of work for you.

